American_Kestrel.Description:
Tiny falcon with rufous-brown back and blue-gray wings. Males have blue wing coverts;
females lack this feature. Both have distinctive facial marks resembling a mustache.
Underparts appear buff to white with sparse markings.

Bachmans_Warbler.Behavior:
Forages in dense swamp vegetation. Possibly extinct;
last confirmed in early 1960s.
One of rarest North American birds.

Black_Storm-Petrel.Habitat:
Eastern Pacific off California and Baja California.
Forages over warm Pacific waters;
breeds on Baja islands.

Bulwers_Petrel.Behavior:
Erratic bat-like flight low over water. Breeds on
Atlantic and Pacific tropical islands;
disperses widely.

Chestnut_Munia.Behavior:
Highly gregarious in large flocks. Forages for grass
seeds. Introduced to Hawaii;
very common on several islands.

Eskimo_Curlew.Habitat:
Bred on Arctic tundra of northwest Canada.
Wintered in South America. Once very common;
now possibly extinct.

Eurasian_Whimbrel.Behavior:
Forages on mudflats and rocky shores for crabs and
invertebrates. Rare vagrant to North America;
white rump distinguishes it from Hudsonian Whimbrel.

European_Turtle-Dove.Behavior:
Feeds on seeds and plant material on ground. Long-
distance migrant. Rare vagrant to North America;
most records from Atlantic coast islands.

Fork-tailed_Storm-Petrel.Habitat:
North Pacific Ocean. Breeds on offshore islands;
forages over North Pacific, particularly
common in Pacific Northwest.

Hawaii_Akepa.Behavior:
Uses crossed bill to twist open ohia buds for
nectar and insects. Endangered;
declining on Big Island.

Hen_Harrier.Behavior:
Hunts small mammals and birds low over open fields.
Breeds in Europe and Asia;
Old World population of Northern Harrier.

Ivory-billed_Woodpecker.Habitat:
Old-growth bottomland hardwood forest. Possibly extinct.
Former range southeastern US;
any recent sightings unconfirmed.

Kentish_Plover.Best Ways to Identify:
Incomplete breast band with side patches. Sandy-
brown plumage. Very similar to Snowy Plover;
identified by leg color and subtle size.

Key_West_Quail-Dove.Behavior:
Walks on forest floor foraging for seeds and
fruit. Secretive. Historically bred in Florida Keys;
now rare vagrant from Bahamas.

Laysan_Duck.Behavior:
Feeds on brine flies, shrimp, and plants.
Highly terrestrial. One of world's rarest ducks;
only about 1,000 remain on Hawaiian atolls.

Masked_Duck.Habitat:
Freshwater ponds and marshes with dense floating
vegetation. South Texas and south Florida;
wanders from Mexico and Caribbean.

Mexican_Duck.Behavior:
Dabbles in freshwater for aquatic plants and
invertebrates. Forms mixed flocks with Mallards;
hybridization common in southwestern US.

Monk_Parakeet.Behavior:
Only parrot that builds stick nests. Forms large colonies.
Established in many US cities;
expanding range.

Nazca_Booby.Habitat:
Eastern tropical Pacific. Breeds Galapagos Islands;
rare vagrant to Pacific coast of
North America.

Ou.Behavior:
Fed on fruit, seeds, and buds. Possibly extinct;
no confirmed records since
the 1980s.

Passenger_Pigeon.Habitat:
Deciduous forests of eastern North America, particularly
beech and oak forests. Migratory;
moved with food sources. Now extinct.

Piratic_Flycatcher.Behavior:
Pirates nest cavities from other species. Migratory;
rare vagrant to North American
Atlantic and Gulf coasts.

Providence_Petrel.Behavior:
Breeds on Lord Howe and Norfolk Islands. Forages
North Pacific in non-breeding season;
occasional vagrant to Pacific coast.

Red-crested_Cardinal.Habitat:
Open areas, gardens, and parks. Introduced to Hawaii;
very common on several
islands.

Red-footed_Falcon.Description:
Small falcon. Male dark gray with red feet and vent;
female rufous with gray back and
barred underparts. About 11 inches.

Rose-throated_Becard.Habitat:
Riparian sycamore and cottonwood canyon forest in
southeastern Arizona. Summer visitor;
nests along Sonoita Creek.

Scaly-naped_Pigeon.Behavior:
Feeds on fruit in forest canopy. Found in Caribbean;
rare vagrant to Florida Keys. Flies
fast with strong wingbeats.

Short-billed_Gull.Habitat:
Pacific coast tidal flats, harbors, and estuaries.
Breeds in western Canada and Alaska;
winters on Pacific coast.

Short-tailed_Hawk.Behavior:
Soars high and dives on birds. Florida resident;
very local in southern Florida forest.
Rare in Texas and Arizona.

South_Polar_Skua.Behavior:
Pirates food from other seabirds. Breeds in Antarctic;
migrates to northern oceans. Seen offshore
on both coasts.

Spoon-billed_Sandpiper.Best Ways to Identify:
Spatula-shaped bill tip absolutely diagnostic. Breeding
adult has reddish face. Critically endangered;
any sighting is exceptional.

Spotted_Dove.Behavior:
Forages on ground for seeds and grain. Found in
pairs or small flocks. Introduced from Asia;
established in southern California and Hawaii.

Swinhoes_White-eye.Similar Looking Birds:
Warbling White-eye (Japanese White-eye) nearly identical;
same group. No other bird
has white eye ring in range.

Tufted_Flycatcher.Behavior:
Sallies for insects from exposed perch. Mexican species;
rare vagrant to southern Arizona
and Texas.

Waved_Albatross.Habitat:
Eastern Pacific off South America. Breeds Galapagos;
very rare vagrant to Pacific coast
of North America.

Western_Cattle-Egret.Behavior:
Follows cattle and large animals to catch disturbed
insects. Highly successful colonizer;
spread from Africa to Americas.

Western_Marsh_Harrier.Description:
Large harrier. Male brown with gray wings and tail;
female dark brown with cream crown
and throat. About 22 inches.

White-rumped_Shama.Behavior:
Superb singer with rich, varied song. Bold and
curious. Introduced to Hawaii;
competes with native species.

White-tipped_Dove.Behavior:
Walks on ground foraging for seeds and fruit.
Relatively tame. Resident in southern Texas;
common in Central and South America.

White_Ibis.Behavior:
Forages in shallow water and mudflats. Highly gregarious;
flocks rest and nest in trees.
Common in southeastern US.

Wilsons_Storm-Petrel.Behavior:
Very abundant seabird worldwide. Patters on water
surface feeding. Breeds in Antarctic;
migrates north in summer.

Yellow_Bittern.Behavior:
Stalks prey in reeds like other bitterns. Asian species;
very rare vagrant to Alaska and
Hawaiian Islands.

Zinos_Petrel.Habitat:
North Atlantic Ocean. Extremely rare vagrant. Breeds
only on central Madeira mountains;
critically endangered.
